    {
      "id": 93648,
      "name": "Edwin",
      "username": "Edwin",
      "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/e/73ab20/{size}.png",
      "created_at": "2014-02-25T06:49:25.000Z",
      "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"Ameena\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/ameena/48/1907_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Ameena:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Yeah that was what we thought - the cards are in play therefore they would finish resolving even though it's no longer the Scholar's Play phase any more.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>I agree that this makes sense.&nbsp; Broccoli touched on the idea that it isn't necessarily Scholar's turn when DDA gets played, therefore if KWTHF doesn't stop him from continuing the resolution when it's Argent's turn, why would it do so when this happens during his own turn?&nbsp; Also if this were the case, it would mean that the order in which DDA plays were resolved would be hugely significant…you'd always want to have Scholar go last to avoid this risk, and I don't think it's consistent with the overall style of SOTM to make it THAT important which order each player does their DDA play in.</p>",
      "post_number": 8,
      "post_type": 1,
      "updated_at": "2021-02-07T03:19:56.296Z",
      "reply_count": 0,
      "reply_to_post_number": null,
